<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diamond Sutra of Dunhuang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">868</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Dunhuang Diamond Sutra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Diamond Sutra of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> is a Chinese woodblock-printed copy of the Diamond Sutra, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> Buddhist scripture, dated to 868 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. Found in 1900 in the sealed library cave of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mogao-caves">Mogao Caves</a> near Dunhuang and acquired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurel-stein">Aurel Stein</a> in 1907, it is the oldest surviving dated printed book. It is held by the British Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p></div>
